There isn't one bug here – there are several independent ones, and any one of them is enough to hang a game. The good news is they're all in the emitter, not in the runner/trampoline, and the log actually matches them nicely (only ARM fallbacks are ever logged, both CPUs end up in tight ARM spin loops with no fallbacks → the Thumb code the ARM7 runs went off the rails silently, and the ARM9 is waiting for something that never happens).

In order of severity:
1. The Thumb dispatcher is off by one nibble (fatal for all Thumb code)

dispThumb switches on (op>>12)&0xF, but cases 0x1–0x5 are labelled as if it were op>>13:
h (op>>12)	Real encoding	What your code does
0x1	ASR imm / ADD‑SUB reg,imm3	emitT_imm8 → compiles as ADD/SUB imm8 to the wrong register
0x2,0x3	MOV/CMP/ADD/SUB imm8	ALU / hi‑reg / LDR‑PC → garbage
0x4	ALU / hi‑reg / LDR PC	emitT_memReg → random loads/stores

Nothing falls back, it just silently computes garbage – which is exactly why there is no thumb FB line in your log. The GBA BIOS logo is ARM code; the game's main() is Thumb → black screen. NDS ARM7 SDK code is mostly Thumb → ARM7 wanders into a loop, ARM9 waits for IPC forever.

C++

static bool dispThumb(Ctx& ctx,uint16_t op,uint32_t curPC){
    switch((op>>12)&0xF){
        case 0x0: case 0x1:
            if(((op>>11)&3)!=3) return emitT_shifts(ctx,op);   // LSL/LSR/ASR #imm
            return emitT_addSub3(ctx,op);                       // ADD/SUB Rd,Rs,Rn/#imm3
        case 0x2: case 0x3: return emitT_imm8(ctx,op);          // MOV/CMP/ADD/SUB #imm8
        case 0x4: {
            uint8_t b=(op>>10)&3;
            if(b==0) return emitT_alu  (ctx,op);
            if(b==1) return emitT_hiReg(ctx,op,curPC);
            return emitT_ldrPc(ctx,op,curPC);
        }
        case 0x5: return emitT_memReg(ctx,op);
        case 0x6: case 0x7: case 0x8: return emitT_memImm(ctx,op);
        case 0x9: return emitT_spLoad (ctx,op,curPC);
        case 0xA: return emitT_addSpPc(ctx,op,curPC);
        case 0xB:
            if((op&0xFF00)==0xB000) return emitT_addSpPc(ctx,op,curPC); // ADD/SUB SP,#imm7
            if((op&0xF600)==0xB400) return emitT_pushPop(ctx,op,curPC); // PUSH/POP
            return false;
        case 0xC: return emitT_ldmStm(ctx,op);
        case 0xD: case 0xE: return emitT_branch(ctx,op,curPC);
        default:  return false;
    }
}

2. SUB SP,#imm is compiled as ADD SP,#imm

Format 13 is 1011 0000 S imm7; the sign is bit 7, not (op>>8)&0xF==1. Every Thumb function prologue is wrong.

