3. C and V flags are never set (wrong rlwinm mask bit numbering)

rlwinm MB/ME use big‑endian numbering (bit 0 = MSB). You got that right in setNZ, sLslI etc., but not in the C/V helpers:

    setC_xer: rlwinm TA,TA,0,29,29 keeps 0x00000004, not XER[CA] (0x20000000 = BE bit 2).
    setC_imm: same mask bug.
    setV_add/sub: rlwinm TE,TE,29,28,28 keeps 0x8, not V (0x10000000 = BE bit 3).

Net effect: every CMP/SUBS/ADDS/LSLS… leaves C=0,V=0. BCS/BHS/BHI never taken, BCC/BLO/BLS always taken → infinite loops in any bounded loop / memcpy / compare.

On top of that, setNZ and setC_xer use TA as scratch, and every caller then does setV_*(res, ..., TA) – so even with the mask fixed V would read a clobbered operand. Switch the scratch to TG (r9, never live across these):

